{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership","id":1348783,"title":"Codifying Leadership","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-15T20:47:57-08:00","id":1348783,"title":"Codifying Leadership","content":"I wanted to make a simple system that slots into the current leadership system and charms that will help codify what\u2019s possible, and give some guidelines for projects. I\u2019d like to start by introducing a few concepts.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Committed Resource Dots </span><br /> <br /> Resources doesn\u2019t represent a total amount of wealth, but rather a revenue stream. This means that spending resource dots doesn\u2019t make a lot of sense, you don\u2019t give up some of your hourly wage to buy things. Instead resource dots will be committed at the start of each project and will be unavailable for other projects for the duration. The number of resource dots committed to a project must be equal to the scope of the project.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking if the character is in a large city, or undertaking a project using locally available resources and people, like construction timber and carpenters in an eastern city, only the Resources merit is required to begin a project. If, for example, the character wanted to start a project requiring a massive amount of wood and carpenters in the middle of a southern desert, they would first have to complete projects forming supply chains for these resources.<br /> <br /> It is also possible to replace the resource costs with a committed Backing background, in which case the Backing dots count as one dot rating higher for the purposes of the project, although the project then belongs to the organization, not the character.<br /> <br /> If a character wishes to start a project with a greater scope than she has resources, she may attempt to raise temporary funds. This is can be accomplished by completing a project of scope equal to her current resources in order to gain a temporary dot for the purposes of the project. If she wishes to attempt a project with a scope several dots above her current rating, consecutive projects to raise funds may be taken.<br /> <br /> Raising funds can also be a dramatic action, and need not use the project system at all. Securing a wealthy donor at a gala, or stealing talents of jade from a Treasure Fleet ship can easily fund a project, as well as provide an adventure.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Project Scope </span><br /> <br /> A project\u2019s scope is a measurement of how long a project will take, how many resources it will consume, and how difficult it is to manage.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope 1:</b> Projects of this scope are relatively small. Constructing a barn and stable, starting a local group town watch of 10-15 members, or negotiating a purchasing deal between businesses. Generally these are projects that can be commissioned by landowners, and have a completion time of 1-2 weeks.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope 2:</b> These projects start to get larger and more complicated. Starting a small business like a tea house from scratch, building a house or sailboat, growing a small town watch into a group of 50 guards. They often take a month or two.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope 3:</b> Projects only able to be easily undertaken by nobility and merchant princes. Constructing a sailing ship, growing the town guard into a small army of over a hundred law enforcers, building a mansion or villa. These projects tend to take several months, on the order of 4-6.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope 4:</b> Projects of this level tend to be large and complex affairs. Negotiating the trade between small nations, constructing a drydock to build ships in, forming an elite cadre of 100 warriors, spreading a cult throughout an entire nation. They take most of a year to complete.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope 5:</b> These projects are beyond the reach of all but the wealthiest individuals, and rulers of nations, and fittingly these projects are often on that scale. Creating not just one drydock but an entire harbor of shipyards, organizing an army of thousands, constructing sprawling fortress compounds and redoubts, or creating trade deals that govern the motions of ships heavy with wealth. These projects take years.<br /> <br /> <b>Scope N/A:</b> Truly massive projects, beyond the scope of even the wealthiest of individuals to finance. Building a wall around the boarder of an entire kingdom, damming a mighty river to form a huge man-made lake, constructing a great pyramid to bury the pharaoh. They take several years if not decades to complete.<br /> <br /> <br /> In general if the project is increasing one of the character's story merits, the scope should be close to the level that the merit is being raised to. For merits like cult or followers, where there is a kind of continuity that grows from a few people to more, to even more, each level must be taken in turn. A massive cult does not simply spring into existence from nowhere! It may even require other projects or adventures to be taken in between increasing merits. For example growing a single teahouse into a large network of teahouses may require a negotiation with the character's tea supplier.<br /> <br /> Scope may also influence the number of failure conditions that arise. A project to rescind a poaching law that protects the local forest may have to deal with angry woodland spirits sending messages of disfavor, or it may have no failure conditions at all. On the other hand a project like constructing a legendarily large pyramid is almost certain to have multiple complications. The number of complications with a project should rarely exceed the scope, however.<br /> <br /> Scope limits the number of projects a character can be managing at one time, only one project of each level of scope can be progressing at a time.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Subordinates</span><br /> <br /> Some characters may be part of large bureaucratic hierarchies and have subordinates who are capable of managing projects on their own. If that is the case, the main character can delegate an underling to begin a project, but may not use any of her own charms or abilities to aid in its completion, unless they specify otherwise, like Bestow the Saffron Mantle. Projects being worked on by subordinates do not count against the limit of the main character's ongoing projects. Let common sense dictate how many projects a character may be overseeing through their staff.<br /> <br /> Staff members may also temporarily take over a project that the main character is running. If, for example, the character needed to take three months off to join her circle in search of an artifact, a staff member may take over, however this will almost certainly cause some form of complication.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Alternative Rule: Extended Roll</span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Alternatively instead of using common-sense guidelines for project time completion, they can be completed with extended rolls, often using (Intelligence + Bureaucracy). Rather than being a guideline for how to use the current rules, this modifies how the system works. For this, the projects have an interval of (1 week x scope), and a difficulty equal to (Scope/2) rounded up. The target number for projects are as follows:<br /> <br /> Scope 1: 5<br /> Scope 2: 15<br /> Scope 3: 30<br /> Scope 4: 45<br /> Scope 5: 75<br /> Scope NA: 100<br /> <br /> <br /> When using this system and failure conditions arise, the next roll to advance the extended action can not be taken until the failure condition has been resolved.<br /> <br /> It is also possible for subordinates to assume control of the project and make rolls using their own abilities while their superior is away. Likewise, assuming the superior has the project slots available, she may assume control of a foundering project that her subordinate is struggling with to complete it faster. It is recommended to be lenient about adding additional complications using this rule, as the reduced effectiveness of the subordinate will likely be punishing enough.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It's a system I might use myself if anyone tends towards leadership roles in my game. Is there any glaring flaw that I missed or, maybe something unclear in the execution?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T05:02:41-08:00","id":1348832,"title":"","content":"I really like this. It's exactly the sort of sweet and simple framework that was lacking from the book.<br /> <br /> My only suggestion would be to tie in spells and charms somehow. Sanctaphrax's crafting overhaul (<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/797631-why-craft-sucks-how-i-fixed-it\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...how-i-fixed-it</a>) had a line about gaining small bonuses for having a &quot;<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial, sans-serif\">Charm, spell, anima power, or other magical ability that's related to the Artifact;&quot; I think something similar would make sense here. Perhaps one auto-success on the extended roll for every relevant supernatural power?</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12314-grod_the_giant","name":"Grod_the_giant","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12314_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T10:27:08-08:00","id":1348884,"title":"","content":"Believe it or not I thought of exactly that kind of thing, but I could only think of a good way to implement it in the extended roll rule, either an auto-success or added dice or something, which I didn\u2019t really like because some people might not like the idea of changing things from the book. If you do that though don\u2019t let\u2019s the bonuses from multiple charms stack, it\u2019s not good for the game for an exalted bureaucrat you assume the role of leader of a nation and three days later they have a Great Wall surrounding it, an army of ten thousand elite soldiers guarding it, and crystal clear water flowing through public bathhouses where there used to be only pig farms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T11:05:43-08:00","id":1348889,"title":"","content":"I'm really interested in this I promise to answer later","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T11:59:43-08:00","id":1348895,"title":"","content":"Overall, I definitely like the idea. Some suggestions, though.<br /> <br /> First, I think the scope should, rather than going from 5 to just NA, instead continue up (Scope 6, 7, etc.). While there's certainly projects that exceed what an individual can finance, I think the odds of a circle of Exalts getting into a position where they can command greater-than-personal resources is so high that projects they will be attempting at that level need more definition. <br /> <br /> Second, I'd also like to see complications be given scope levels as well. Not all complications are created equal, and I think it provides more interesting storytelling opportunities if the ST can throw complications that slow down a project, but not necessarily prevent it entirely, meaning that the players have to choose which complications on which projects they have to deal with, and which have to be left to just delay their work.<br /> <br /> Third, I'd suggest developing some rules for how committing more Resources than you need for a project can help. If a master merchant with Resources 5 pours <i>all</i> of that into building a barn, that should mean that the job gets finished faster, with better quality, or simply steamrolls some complications. I wouldn't recommend <i>completely</i> eliminating complications for this, but reducing their scope, as per the above suggestion, might make sense. <br /> <br /> Finally, I'd also come up with some mechanism for allowing other appropriate background merits to be committed to a project to boost it in some way. Probably not a direct one-dot-to-one relationship to Resources, but it should be possible, if you have Followers who happen to be a coterie of scribes from the Imperial Bureaucracy, to add them to the project to duplicate all the books in a great library, and get better results.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T13:01:28-08:00","id":1348910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348895#post1348895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Overall, I definitely like the idea. Some suggestions, though.<br /> <br /> First, I think the scope should, rather than going from 5 to just NA, instead continue up (Scope 6, 7, etc.). While there's certainly projects that exceed what an individual can finance, I think the odds of a circle of Exalts getting into a position where they can command greater-than-personal resources is so high that projects they will be attempting at that level need more definition.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not quite sure there's enough meaningful difference between building a harbor full of shipyards and constructing the Great Wall. I mean, there is clearly a huge difference, but I think that it fits with the way the resources system is designed, where each dot represents more of a leap than the last.<br /> <br /> I would also say that there are projects beyond scope N/A, like building a wall around all of Creation, but that probably doesn't need to be codified as that is deserving of a massive high essence campaign all on its own.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348895#post1348895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second, I'd also like to see complications be given scope levels as well. Not all complications are created equal, and I think it provides more interesting storytelling opportunities if the ST can throw complications that slow down a project, but not necessarily prevent it entirely, meaning that the players have to choose which complications on which projects they have to deal with, and which have to be left to just delay their work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe some example complications? Or what about complications that have a value, and the combined value of complications shouldn't exceed the scope of the project? I'm trying to think of a way to do complications that just delay work without making it too intricate, because if there's one thing Exalted doesn't need it's another really finicky system. It might be enough to add in to complications that some merely cause long delays? For people using the rolling system it could count as skipping the next X number of rolls, maybe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348895#post1348895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Third, I'd suggest developing some rules for how committing more Resources than you need for a project can help. If a master merchant with Resources 5 pours <i>all</i> of that into building a barn, that should mean that the job gets finished faster, with better quality, or simply steamrolls some complications. I wouldn't recommend <i>completely</i> eliminating complications for this, but reducing their scope, as per the above suggestion, might make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh right, I actually also DID think of this but forgot to put it in. Committing Resources equal to one dot higher than the project would normally require treats it as one Scope lower than it is, although that's the limit. It doesn't really matter how much money you throw at a problem you simply aren't building a blue water sailing ship in a single night, that's the domain of sorcery or maybe highly specialized charms.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348895#post1348895\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Finally, I'd also come up with some mechanism for allowing other appropriate background merits to be committed to a project to boost it in some way. Probably not a direct one-dot-to-one relationship to Resources, but it should be possible, if you have Followers who happen to be a coterie of scribes from the Imperial Bureaucracy, to add them to the project to duplicate all the books in a great library, and get better results.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good idea. What if you had an appropriate merit like 50 followers who are carpenters and you wanted to build a house, or bureaucrats and were negotiating a deal, it would count as one resource level lower, but keep the scope. So you don't need to pay the carpenters, but they still need wood, nails and replacement tools, so there's still some cost, but it's one less thing.<br /> <br /> It would have to be at something of an appropriate level though, having one dot of followers (Carpenters) doesn't drop building a nation sized palisade wall down from N/A to 5.<br /> <br /> Although I have to say it's making me wonder how those carpenters are feeding themselves. Maybe it counts as them doing their day job and then spending the rest of their free time doing your work? Or maybe it's just something glossed over.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T13:45:13-08:00","id":1348921,"title":"","content":"Speaking from personal experience.<br /> <br /> I don't think (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) is a good default pool for furthering projects via the Extended Roll. It suggests the PC is moving the plan forward entirely based upon their superior planning, so it's going to be really hard for the Storyteller to introduce logical consequences for the project.<br /> <br /> A character who rolled 100 successes on an (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) roll to dam a river and gets caught off guard by the ecological consequences kinda looks like a fucking idiot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T14:36:48-08:00","id":1348929,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348921#post1348921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking from personal experience.<br /> <br /> I don't think (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) is a good default pool for furthering projects via the Extended Roll. It suggests the PC is moving the plan forward entirely based upon their superior planning, so it's going to be really hard for the Storyteller to introduce logical consequences for the project.<br /> <br /> A character who rolled 100 successes on an (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) roll to dam a river and gets caught off guard by the ecological consequences kinda looks like a fucking idiot.</div> </div> </div> </div>What if it was fluffed more like the immeasurable genius of the project planner discovered a problem before it started? Like if there was a roll to advance diverting a river the complication might arise that an ecological disaster <i>would </i>happen if it went forward as currently planned due to a barely perceptible grade in the soil, and that needs to be fixed before the floodgates open.<br /> <br /> Or even just being blindsided, the planner actually did account for that change in elevation, but adversarial earth spirits took it upon themselves to sabotage this human intrusion into their domain, and that needs to be dealt with or else further sabotage is likely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T15:02:05-08:00","id":1348938,"title":"","content":"Personally, I think Charisma's a better corresponding attribute in most instances. It's less about being right it's about selling your peers and subordinates on the goal ahead. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T15:29:08-08:00","id":1348945,"title":"","content":"This is difficult. I agree that leadership is almost 100% about directing and inspiring people, but there should be something about coming up with a good plan. Improving operational efficiency is much more about intelligence than leadership. Adjusting work shifts to have smooth transitions, cutting through red tape by knowing about legal shortcuts, organizing transports so that they're never moving while empty and are always doing something useful, stuff like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T15:47:29-08:00","id":1348956,"title":"","content":"Really this should be a two person thing, the leader and the organizer. I\u2019m not sure if that\u2019s a good level of complexity though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T18:16:22-08:00","id":1348973,"title":"","content":"I've always assumed Bureaucracy rolls for leading organizations used charisma or manipulation, depending on leadership style.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11917-aranfan","name":"Aranfan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11917_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"503"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-16T20:13:55-08:00","id":1348988,"title":"","content":"Personally I'd stick with Intelligence - no matter how good the speeches about the dam are, if it isn't logically planned there's still going to be a disaster. But you could go to (lower of Int or appropriate Social Attribute), with the possibility of a second character providing one of the values if they're leading as a team?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2433-highpriest","name":"HighPriest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2433_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-17T01:56:14-08:00","id":1349032,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348945#post1348945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is difficult. I agree that leadership is almost 100% about directing and inspiring people, but there should be something about coming up with a good plan. Improving operational efficiency is much more about intelligence than leadership. Adjusting work shifts to have smooth transitions, cutting through red tape by knowing about legal shortcuts, organizing transports so that they're never moving while empty and are always doing something useful, stuff like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say the need to have plausible answers that work within the framework of the existing rules is why you're rolling Bureaucracy with it instead of Presence or Performance which would have no difficulty assembling mobs of people who really want a dam now but have no idea how to achieve it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-17T02:13:58-08:00","id":1349034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348921#post1348921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking from personal experience.<br /> <br /> I don't think (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) is a good default pool for furthering projects via the Extended Roll. It suggests the PC is moving the plan forward entirely based upon their superior planning, so it's going to be really hard for the Storyteller to introduce logical consequences for the project.<br /> <br /> A character who rolled 100 successes on an (Intelligence + Bureaucracy) roll to dam a river and gets caught off guard by the ecological consequences kinda looks like a fucking idiot.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://archives.erfworld.com/Book_1/137/thumb?fb\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\"> The Battle for Gobwin Knob - Episode 124 </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\"> https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> The Battle for Gobwin Knob - Episode 124 </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> No-one knows how to make a pencil. Even with Exalted's themes around individual heroes saving the world, I don't think that's unreasonable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-17T14:11:30-08:00","id":1349163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1348910#post1348910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not quite sure there's enough meaningful difference between building a harbor full of shipyards and constructing the Great Wall. I mean, there is clearly a huge difference, but I think that it fits with the way the resources system is designed, where each dot represents more of a leap than the last.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I realize that the system is basically exponential, I still worry that once the PCs get to the point of ruling a significant chunk of territory in their own right, it will be a matter of &quot;all the projects are now NA level&quot;, and that could get both boring, and irritating to the ST if there aren't useful guidelines for different levels of projects at this scale.<br /> <br /> What I'd suggest is something along these lines. Have Scope NA, written up as<br /> <br /> &quot;Scope N/A: Truly massive projects, beyond the scope of even the wealthiest of individuals to finance. At this level, such projects require mobilizing the resources of entire regions of Creation, which means the initiator either has to convince the rulers of such a region to back them, or be the ruler themselves. A project undertaken solely by a region of a size that matches the requirement will usually involve a notable fraction of the populace making sacrifices, whether in the form of labor, wealth, or other resources. As such, complications at this scope frequently involve unwillingness on the part of the populace to support the project. Since these sorts of projects can often serve as the centerpiece of entire chronicles, the normal guidelines for complications don't apply - the Storyteller can and should continue to add complications to projects of NA scope for as long as that would be narratively satisfying. The following levels of Scope NA are available:<br /> <br /> Scope NA (City): Such projects need the resources of a community of significant size (usually 10,000 or more people). They can include such endeavors as creating a massive shipyard capable of mass-producing warships on a production line, damming a major river to form a huge reservoir, or opening and thoroughly exploiting a resource such as a forest or a complex of mines that has not previously been utilized. Projects at this scale can take years or decades to complete.<br /> <br /> Scope NA (Provincial): At this level, a project needs the resources of a large region, containing multiple cities, to have a hope of completion (a single huge metropolis, on the scale of Nexus or the Imperial City, might suffice). They typically take multiple decades to finish. Examples include building a truly monumental tomb for a pharaoh, constructing an entirely new city, or forging a trading empire that links multiple rich areas of Creation together in a network that enriches all participants (though particularly the originator). <br /> <br /> Scope NA (National): A national-scope project will demand the resources of an entire country. Such massive projects include fortifications that protect entire regions, monumental architecture that will go down in history as a wonder of Creation, or the herculean task of setting up a functioning bureaucracy for a new nation, starting completely from scratch. Projects of this scope require multiple decades or centuries to finish.<br /> <br /> Scope NA (Imperial): Projects of imperial scope require the resources of multiple nations, all yoked together and pulling as one. In the Age of Sorrows, only the Realm is capable of projects of this scope, and even they almost never initiate them. Examples of imperial projects include converting a formerly-wilderness region into a fully functional nation-sized area, building the absolutely grandest architecture possible, or creating a system of economic exploitation that draws on the resources of many peripheral nations to enrich the imperial center, without absolutely devastating the periphery in short order. These projects require multiple centuries.<br /> <br /> Scope NA (Universal): At this level, a project demands the resources of entire plane of existence. Only all of Creation working together, the united Nations of Autochthonia, or the entire Underworld could accomplish something of this level. Such projects require many centuries or millennia to finish. Examples include setting up defenses that protect a significant chunk of the entire dimension, or radically reorganizing the economy of the whole world such that totally different groups now benefit. No polity in Creation in the Age of Sorrows is capable of projects of this scope, and even in the Age of Glory when the Exalted Host was more unified, they were extremely rare.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe some example complications? Or what about complications that have a value, and the combined value of complications shouldn't exceed the scope of the project? I'm trying to think of a way to do complications that just delay work without making it too intricate, because if there's one thing Exalted doesn't need it's another really finicky system. It might be enough to add in to complications that some merely cause long delays?</div> </div> </div> </div>Example complications would be nice, for sure. I do agree that there should be some guidelines for limiting complications, though I'm not sure they need to be quite so codified as giving them a value and totaling that up. I think I'd prefer to say that a few complications of a given level just combine into one more significant one, and then say the ST should introduce only a certain number of complications per project. Here's a suggestion for complication levels:<br /> <br /> Minor Complications: Complications of this level aren't worth bothering with - they reduce a project's efficiency and make it a bit more annoying to finish, but don't really threaten to prevent its ultimate completion. Examples include the sort of petty corruption and mistakes that dog practically every endeavor. A good roll when putting together a project or the use of charms like Speed the Wheels can be stunted as virtually eliminating complications of this level.<br /> <br /> Significant Complications: These sorts of hindrances don't necessarily prevent a project from finishing, but they certainly make it cost more or take much longer if they aren't addressed. Significant complications can include a major supply of material needed for the project being cut off, heavy embezzlement or other corruption, or active hindrance by other, less-powerful, groups that don't want the project to go forward. A project experiencing a significant complication will usually take time to complete appropriate to the next level of scope. A ship-construction project, for example, when the complication of the best source of timber for its hull being cut off appears, could take most of a year to complete instead of only the few months it would normally require. <br /> <br /> Critical Complications: At this level, complications not only delay projects, they threaten to prevent them ever being completed. Corruption that drains most of the capital invested, destruction or other forms of denial of basic infrastructure, or the opposition of figures with the authority to prevent the project, are all forms that complications at this level might take. Until a critical complication is resolved, a project cannot be completed, though progress can sometimes continue depending on the exact nature of both project and complication, so that completion is relatively swift once the complication <i>is</i> removed.<br /> <br /> Overwhelming Complications: An overwhelming complication is one so great that it prevents both completion and any effective progress. Examples of such complications include disasters that destroy all significant infrastructure and material for the project, corruption that removes, not only the wealth invested in the project, but also the overall resources of the individual or polity that finances it, or the opposition of political figures with the power of life or death, usually to both the project itself and the entity supporting it. Overwhelming complications must always be dealt with before any progress can be made, and if this is not done swiftly, they often effectively become impossible to overcome, meaning the same project can't be attempted in the same region at all.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh right, I actually also DID think of this but forgot to put it in. Committing Resources equal to one dot higher than the project would normally require treats it as one Scope lower than it is, although that's the limit. It doesn't really matter how much money you throw at a problem you simply aren't building a blue water sailing ship in a single night, that's the domain of sorcery or maybe highly specialized charms.</div> </div> </div> </div>I definitely agree that there's a limit to how much resources you can throw at a project practically. Based on my suggestions above, I'd recommend allowing someone to spend up to 2 Resources more than required (or, if you're powerful enough, requisitioning the resources of a higher-level region), and letting the project organizer choose to reduce the Scope by 1, leaving a dot as a buffer against significant complications (each dot spent this way means ignoring the delaying effects of one significant complication), or, if they spend two full dots of Resources, effectively immunizing the project against a reasonably wide category of critical complication (so, for instance, the organizer could declare that they're spending the extra resources on making sure they have lots of redundant infrastructure, thus ensuring that destroying that doesn't count as a critical complication). <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would have to be at something of an appropriate level though, having one dot of followers (Carpenters) doesn't drop building a nation sized palisade wall down from N/A to 5.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, that's definitely true. I'd say offhand that Followers count as Resources equal to their dot level if they've been defined as having appropriate skills (100 carpenters can probably build a ship), but as two dots lower (but 2 or 3-dot Followers always count as at least 1 dot) for projects that they aren't appropriate for. Multiple sets of Followers don't count as more Resource dots, though - you can't stack 5 3-dot Followers merits to get the equivalent of Resources 15. You can, at best, get a 1-dot boost if you commit 10 sets of Followers of the same dot level. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Although I have to say it's making me wonder how those carpenters are feeding themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I feel that Followers and similar merits (Command, Retainer, etc.) should come with the assumption that you have sufficient income or other resources to feed and pay them, at least in normal circumstances. You could be a merchant prince with several sets of Followers and a Command, for instance, but no Resources - the assumption would just be that pretty much all your income from your merchant activities was going to support your entourage, and didn't leave you any disposable income left over. Note that is only for &quot;normal use&quot;, whatever that means for the people in question. If you're going to be having your hundred Followers shipped across the Dreaming Sea to help on a project somewhere far from their home, you'd best have the Resources to pay for their travel expenses, or at least the spare treasure.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T04:19:11-08:00","id":1349309,"title":"","content":"Well I guess Kelly Pedersen came with all the points I had in mind but with better solutions so I'm quite happy to be late here !<br /> <br /> I like the idea of different levels of complications, it helps to fix what the player/character would like to fix now or what they can afford to let go.<br /> <br /> I have one question though : Usually people (in my games at least) have Influence. How would it act in these guidelines ? Because to me it is clearly not something enough on its own (I don't feel you are supposed to say &quot;Want to build a war boat and you are the king of the Region with 0 ressources ? Yeah It'll take some months&quot;). Any ideas about it ? I feel like Influence should be some kind of enabler or something, or maybe something that can help against Complications ?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T07:57:13-08:00","id":1349339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349163#post1349163\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I realize that the system is basically exponential, I still worry that once the PCs get to the point of ruling a significant chunk of territory in their own right, it will be a matter of &amp;quot;all the projects are now NA level&amp;quot;, and that could get both boring, and irritating to the ST if there aren't useful guidelines for different levels of projects at this scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is true, but I feel like at that point we\u2019re beyond making this an outgrowth of the projects system. Building something on the scale of the Realm Defense Grid is something that should be the focus of an entire campaign. It\u2019s like how the combat system doesn\u2019t really account for battles involving tens of millions of combatants or how to battle a primordial. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349163#post1349163\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Example complications would be nice, for sure. I do agree that there should be some guidelines for limiting complications, though I'm not sure they need to be quite so codified as giving them a value and totaling that up. I think I'd prefer to say that a few complications of a given level just combine into one more significant one, and then say the ST should introduce only a certain number of complications per project. Here's a suggestion for complication levels:</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay I think I\u2019m understanding a bit better. You\u2019re saying you might want complications that don\u2019t necessarily require action to keep the project going, so that maybe if two crop up at once one can be ignored? I mostly ask because, knowing PCs, if there\u2019s a problem with their work they\u2019re going to fix it. Actual humans are often lazy, but PCs tend to be rabid about going on adventures to fix problems. <br /> <br /> The different levels of complications might also imply a different amount of merits you could commit to fixing them, as well. Like if an entire circle of Abyssals was making a concerted effort to sabotage your project that\u2019s something you may wish to handle personally, but you could send your legion of bureaucrats to cut through the trade negotiation red tape.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349163#post1349163\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, that's definitely true. I'd say offhand that Followers count as Resources equal to their dot level if they've been defined as having appropriate skills (100 carpenters can probably build a ship), but as two dots lower (but 2 or 3-dot Followers always count as at least 1 dot) for projects that they aren't appropriate for. Multiple sets of Followers don't count as more Resource dots, though - you can't stack 5 3-dot Followers merits to get the equivalent of Resources 15. You can, at best, get a 1-dot boost if you commit 10 sets of Followers of the same dot level. </div> </div> </div> </div>I would say due to exponential scaling stacking any merits is probably not appropriate, at least linearly. A circle of 5 PCs with resources 1 each are not equivalent of resources 5.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349163#post1349163\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Personally, I feel that Followers and similar merits (Command, Retainer, etc.) should come with the assumption that you have sufficient income or other resources to feed and pay them, at least in normal circumstances. You could be a merchant prince with several sets of Followers and a Command, for instance, but no Resources - the assumption would just be that pretty much all your income from your merchant activities was going to support your entourage, and didn't leave you any disposable income left over. Note that is only for &amp;quot;normal use&amp;quot;, whatever that means for the people in question. If you're going to be having your hundred Followers shipped across the Dreaming Sea to help on a project somewhere far from their home, you'd best have the Resources to pay for their travel expenses, or at least the spare treasure.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that\u2019s more stuff that might be beyond the scope of a system to codify. You start getting into really really finicky stuff like specific wages and how much of what kind of nutrition they need and you might as well make a new RPG based on project management.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T10:55:14-08:00","id":1349369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349034#post1349034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://archives.erfworld.com/Book_1/137/thumb?fb\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\"> The Battle for Gobwin Knob - Episode 124 </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137\" target=\"_blank\"> https://archives.erfworld.com/Book+1/137 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> The Battle for Gobwin Knob - Episode 124 </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> No-one knows how to make a pencil. Even with Exalted's themes around individual heroes saving the world, I don't think that's unreasonable.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not 100% clear what you're saying because you're depending on the comic to make most of your point.<br /> This isn't about wanting a flawless plan, I firmly believe in consequences but if a project's progress is entirely determined by the strength of its planning it'll be harder for the Storyteller to supply consequences that the player considers reasonable project oversights.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T12:16:34-08:00","id":1349398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349339#post1349339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is true, but I feel like at that point we\u2019re beyond making this an outgrowth of the projects system. Building something on the scale of the Realm Defense Grid is something that should be the focus of an entire campaign.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but, as I pointed out, there are plenty of projects that are below the level of &quot;Realm Defense Grid&quot; but above &quot;Resources 5 to start&quot;. In fact, a starting character can plausibly start on such right out of the gate, given that the Influence merit suggests that 4 dots in it can represent someone who rules a small city, while 5 dots can represent the ruler of a city-state with broader influence. I feel that lumping all the possible government-scale projects into one NA level just isn't that useful. Artifacts can get away with that sort of rating because Artifact NA is much, much harder to reach the level of &quot;coming up regularly in a story&quot;. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay I think I\u2019m understanding a bit better. You\u2019re saying you might want complications that don\u2019t necessarily require action to keep the project going, so that maybe if two crop up at once one can be ignored?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, and to provide more scope for interesting complications that actually stick around and continue to cause problems. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mostly ask because, knowing PCs, if there\u2019s a problem with their work they\u2019re going to fix it. Actual humans are often lazy, but PCs tend to be rabid about going on adventures to fix problems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, that's my experience as well. But that's actually <i>why</i> I think not all complications should totally halt the project. If a complication stops a project the PCs want to do, they'll probably drop other stuff and go fix it, because the tasks they choose themselves are always going to weigh more heavily on them than stuff that's come up incidentally. But if a complication only <i>delays</i> a project, I think it's much more likely that there'll be an actual dilemma. If the PCs' choices are &quot;go and deal with the Guild member who's raising prices for timber and delaying our navy construction&quot; or &quot;go fight the invading army that's coming over the northern border <i>right now</i>&quot;, I think there's a much better chance that they'll choose the latter, whereas if the choice is between &quot;deal with the Guild Factor who's totally blocking our shipbuilding project from proceeding at all&quot; vs. stopping the invasion, they're more likely to go for the former.<br /> <br /> Also, by having lower-level complications available that delay projects without absolutely stopping them, I think the ST can throw more of them at the players without inspiring as much resentment. If the ST says &quot;the following complications have arisen, stopping all three projects. Which one will you deal with, leaving the other two stalled&quot;, that's more likely to make people mad than if the option is &quot;which problem will you deal with first, while the other projects happen more slowly?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The different levels of complications might also imply a different amount of merits you could commit to fixing them, as well. Like if an entire circle of Abyssals was making a concerted effort to sabotage your project that\u2019s something you may wish to handle personally, but you could send your legion of bureaucrats to cut through the trade negotiation red tape.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps, but I'd be very cautious in how much I allowed for that, as opposed to players having to deal with complications more directly. The whole point of the project/complication system in 3e, at least as I understand it, is to ensure that the PCs always have reasons to go out, act, and use their abilities. Systems where the master bureaucrat waves her pen and deploys NPCs, while the PCs sit back sipping age-staving cordial and feeling bored, are to be avoided. I would prefer if things like Followers and other resources were required to be committed preemptively, either when the project begins, or at the very least, at the start of the story, and made it so that complications of an appropriate nature just didn't come up. That way, the ST could still throw complications into the mix, but the PCs would get some ability to narrow down the possibilities of <i>which</i> ones arose, customizing them to their skillsets. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say due to exponential scaling stacking any merits is probably not appropriate, at least linearly. A circle of 5 PCs with resources 1 each are not equivalent of resources 5.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely agreed here. The &quot;combine 10 Followers to increase one dot&quot; thing was just to cover the idea if the players do have 1000 loyal minions handy, they should get a bit more effect than just 100. But I'd say that no Merits could ever be raised more than one dot through stuff like this. 5 PCs might be able to pool their single Resources 1 dots to bankroll a Scope <i>2</i> project, but not more than that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, that\u2019s more stuff that might be beyond the scope of a system to codify.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. My point was that I <i>don't</i> think most Merits like Followers need to worry about being bankrolled. I just don't think it's unreasonable for the ST to require a bit of extra cash if the PCs take the previously garrison-bound troops on campaign, or ask their coterie of scribes to sail from the Imperial City to Medo. But the project system is probably not the place to codify that. It doesn't need to bring up the idea of paying extra resources for other Merits at all, really.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T13:04:38-08:00","id":1349422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349369#post1349369\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not 100% clear what you're saying because you're depending on the comic to make most of your point.<br /> This isn't about wanting a flawless plan, I firmly believe in consequences but if a project's progress is entirely determined by the strength of its planning it'll be harder for the Storyteller to supply consequences that the player considers reasonable project oversights.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm.<br /> <br /> Say I want to build a dam. There's not much more I can do than to plan the project. And I might plan it really well. I roll 100 successes.<br /> <br /> But that doesn't tell me what I don't know.<br /> <br /> I don't know if the people I'm relying on for wood are going to be attacked by the undead. I don't know that the land I plan on flooding is protected by powerful gods. I don't know that there's a First Age city under the river.<br /> <br /> With 100 successes, I could plan for all these contingencies... if I want to pay for all these contingencies. I'm cognisant enough of the risks to know they exist, in the abstract, and I know what steps I should take to mitigate them. But there's a thousand thousand things which could go wrong but almost certainly won't. Planning for them all will slow the project to a snails pace and massively inflate the cost.<br /> <br /> So sure, I know building the dam will flood upriver. But there's a nice basin, and I've chose to build the dam there (with Intelligence, not Charisma). I didn't know that a rare animal the ecosystem depends on lives in that basin, or that the basin has high levels of lye which will be washed away, or that zombies would eat my lumberjacks. I could have found these things out, but there's a reason I want that dam and I don't fancy spending the next forty years investigating every single thing that could possibly go wrong and planning contingencies.<br /> <br /> 100 successes doesn't mean nothing surprises you. It means when zombies eat your lumberjacks, you've got enough money in the slush-fund to hire ghostly loggers. You still need to treat with the undead, which is why it's a complication -- these people just gave their lives for you, now you're ruining their deaths, they want some serious workman's comp.<br /> <br /> Further, I've come up with some pretty darn flawless plans in my life. Every single one of them was fouled up by someone not doing as they told. If you plan a perfect dam but your brother ignores the plan, embezzles all the money, makes the dam hollow and frames you for murder then that's a pretty serious complication.<br /> <br /> If I understand you correctly, your concern is the player thinking that a really good planning roll means nothing can go wrong because they're so smart. If your players consider themselves smart IRL, ask if nothing has ever gone wrong in their life. If they think people not following their plans or something unexpected happening makes them less smart. If your players don't consider themselves smart, and think their life would be perfect if they were more intelligent, then take a moment to validate them as a person and reassure them that even smart people aren't omniscient.<br /> <br /> The Exalted are not omniscient.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/899114-the-official-exalted-forum-introduction-thread?p=899399#post899399\" target=\"_blank\">Hi, I'm JohnDoe244</a>. My posts represent my opinions, not facts.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/531-johndoe244","name":"JohnDoe244","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar531_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2958"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-18T17:16:02-08:00","id":1349467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JohnDoe244</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349422#post1349422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I understand you correctly, your concern is the player thinking that a really good planning roll means nothing can go wrong because they're so smart.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not quite.<br /> My concern is the player feeling that the complications are not an appropriate reflection of the level of planning that went into the project. Some of your own examples would make the character infamous, not because they engaged in a doomed course of action or botched but because they suceeded.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-19T00:34:55-08:00","id":1349514,"title":"","content":"As a note, while that's not Lioness' problem, that is actually generally a problem I have felt with codifying these sort of systems, and I think her argumentst hat it is about much more just getting people to do the thing you want, rather than whether what you want to do &quot;works&quot; or not, is probably a much more reflective view of things, and one which generates potential problems that you would see in these large scale projects. The acutal like, mechanics to make sure something you're attempting is even like, a good idea, I think are just seperate from implementing them, which seems to be a concern noted earlier in the thread.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-20T21:45:01-08:00","id":1349977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kelly Pedersen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349398#post1349398\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps, but I'd be very cautious in how much I allowed for that, as opposed to players having to deal with complications more directly. The whole point of the project/complication system in 3e, at least as I understand it, is to ensure that the PCs always have reasons to go out, act, and use their abilities. Systems where the master bureaucrat waves her pen and deploys NPCs, while the PCs sit back sipping age-staving cordial and feeling bored, are to be avoided. I would prefer if things like Followers and other resources were required to be committed preemptively, either when the project begins, or at the very least, at the start of the story, and made it so that complications of an appropriate nature just didn't come up. That way, the ST could still throw complications into the mix, but the PCs would get some ability to narrow down the possibilities of <i>which</i> ones arose, customizing them to their skillsets.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, bingo, I think I found the cause of our misunderstanding. I was thinking that, for the leadership system to be meaningful, the leader would have to be watching over it pretty much as a full time job, with days off, maybe a vacation here and there, but otherwise pretty much they're managing it. In which case any time not spent dealing with complications is spent in downtime with the leader rolling to try and shave time off of the project completion. Or if you're not rolling just advancing the project.<br /> <br /> That's why I was hesitant about complications you can afford to ignore, the complications are the only time interesting story things are happening so I figured what's the point? But you're thinking it's a system that's running in the background while other stories are being played out, correct?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-21T00:25:05-08:00","id":1349989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349977#post1349977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ah, bingo, I think I found the cause of our misunderstanding. I was thinking that, for the leadership system to be meaningful, the leader would have to be watching over it pretty much as a full time job, with days off, maybe a vacation here and there, but otherwise pretty much they're managing it. In which case any time not spent dealing with complications is spent in downtime with the leader rolling to try and shave time off of the project completion. Or if you're not rolling just advancing the project.<br /> <br /> That's why I was hesitant about complications you can afford to ignore, the complications are the only time interesting story things are happening so I figured what's the point? But you're thinking it's a system that's running in the background while other stories are being played out, correct?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if that was what Kelly Pedersen was thinking but I think that's how a lot of people play in Exalted (at least in my tables). You put your merits and you go do adventures while it's progressing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My homebrew (Leave comments if you want to help improve) : <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1hPx-Q2jlDgAIZX_d0wQWmJkZMN51jas4QNp5meeQjgo/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">A quick recap of all the pools and stats for Quick Exalted 3E characters</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24291-chausse","name":"Chausse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-21T10:45:49-08:00","id":1350052,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349977#post1349977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ah, bingo, I think I found the cause of our misunderstanding. I was thinking that, for the leadership system to be meaningful, the leader would have to be watching over it pretty much as a full time job, with days off, maybe a vacation here and there, but otherwise pretty much they're managing it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I wouldn't use the system if that was the assumption, to be honest. While I have no objection to the idea of the Heroic Project Manager being a viable character concept in Exalted, actually <i>playing out</i> that time, or even just having the other PCs put their activities on hold and go for an extended downtime sequence while the Bureaucracy character handles her projects, doesn't strike me as fun at all. Note how Exalted 3e has handled other extended projects, like artifact crafting or sorcerous workings: they're all explicitly described as stuff you can make progress off-screen on, as long as you've got access to the materials/location. Bureaucracy projects should be the same way, I strongly feel. In fact, the typical project is probably <i>easier</i> to narratively justify that sort of hands-off approach than most projects, since, in all but the smallest projects, the leader isn't the one doing the actual work, they've just provided direction. An upper manager who has to constantly tweak and micro-manage a task is a sign of a <i>poor</i> manager, not a supernally skilled one. The truly great planner can create a scheme that anticipates most problems before they happen, and give her underlings instructions that are easily followed and account for any minor issues that occur. To continue a project's progress while the leader is away, I'd only require the leader to be in somewhat regular communication with the people carrying it out, along the lines of &quot;progress reports delivered via mail once a season&quot;. More immediate communication would only be required in the aftermath of a complication arising and being resolved, at which point some new instructions to get back on track would be useful.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-21T11:16:40-08:00","id":1350066,"title":"","content":"How do you square that with projects that take place at a different timescale to other character\u2019s plans though? If you\u2019re building the Great Wall of Daraheim and it\u2019s projected to take 5 years even with your greatest Solar efforts there will always be the need to insert some downtime otherwise you\u2019d never finish. <br /> <br /> For what I was thinking the extended downtime isn\u2019t very long at all in play. If you\u2019re rolling maybe a roll or two, and if you\u2019re not rolling it\u2019s just the ST skipping to the next complication.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1121-drlovemonkey","name":"DrLoveMonkey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1121_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4736"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-21T12:30:49-08:00","id":1350082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrLoveMonkey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1350066#post1350066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do you square that with projects that take place at a different timescale to other character\u2019s plans though? If you\u2019re building the Great Wall of Daraheim and it\u2019s projected to take 5 years even with your greatest Solar efforts there will always be the need to insert some downtime otherwise you\u2019d never finish.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, a couple of things - first, charms like Speed the Wheels exist, and can often greatly shorten the required time for a project.<br /> <br /> I don't think they can necessarily completely shorten <i>all</i> the time required for a not-purely-bureaucratic project, like building a wall, but they can still shorten it a lot by making sure all the logistics perfectly line up. If the Great Wall of Daraheim would be a Scope N/A (National) level project that would take a century to complete, an Essence 5 Solar with Speed the Wheels could shorten all the required time to arrange supplies and workers, ready on the site to begin building, down to mere minutes. Of course, placing brick on brick takes longer, but probably still not <i>that</i> long, especially if the project leader also arranged for things like shifts working all day and night. <br /> <br /> The second thing is that yes, there's definitely going to be times when it makes sense for there to be a downtime montage - the PC Circle doesn't go on adventures for a year, the Solar bureaucrat works on a variety of project to improve their nation, the Solar general boosts their armies' training, the Solar sorcerer completes a bunch of workings to buff their region, and so forth. In fact, I'd call opportunities like that pretty essential for the pacing of the game. Without them, the PCs just hit Essence 5 way too fast, there's no sense of the grand scope of Creation, and it's very hard to justify seriously threatening responses from other nations or other actors. What I don't like is the idea that any time a bureaucrat starts a project, such downtime is automatically <i>required</i>, or the project leader has to stop working on their projects in order to join their Circle for adventures.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Interested in chatting with other Exalted fans online? Join us on the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/kGsyF5jhdy\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted Discord server</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1533-kelly-pedersen","name":"Kelly Pedersen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"929"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-23T07:07:17-08:00","id":1350435,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1348783-codifying-leadership?p=1349514#post1349514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a note, while that's not Lioness' problem, that is actually generally a problem I have felt with codifying these sort of systems, and I think her argumentst hat it is about much more just getting people to do the thing you want, rather than whether what you want to do &quot;works&quot; or not, is probably a much more reflective view of things, and one which generates potential problems that you would see in these large scale projects. The acutal like, mechanics to make sure something you're attempting is even like, a good idea, I think are just seperate from implementing them, which seems to be a concern noted earlier in the thread.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, to me there's a correlation.<br /> The Storyteller is trusted to provide good information on intelligence (and sensory) based rolls because that's how players interact with the world. So if your series of successful (Intelligence + Bureauracy) rolls for planning means your character accidently built a manor house over an old burial ground then they've kind of betrayed you for the sake of a twist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}